    {
        "text": "RDF has a few quirks due to the fact that it is designed for internet-wide data\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "exchange. The subject, predicate, and object of a triple are often URIs. For example, a\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "predicate might be an URI such as <http://my-company.com/namespace#within> or\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "<http://my-company.com/namespace#lives_in>, rather than just WITHIN or\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "LIVES_IN. The reasoning behind this design is that you should be able to combine\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "your data with someone else\u2019s data, and if they attach a different meaning to the word\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "within or lives_in, you won\u2019t get a conflict because their predicates are actually\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "<http://other.org/foo#within> and <http://other.org/foo#lives_in>.\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "The URL <http://my-company.com/namespace> doesn\u2019t necessarily need to resolve\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "to anything\u2014from RDF\u2019s point of view, it is simply a namespace. To avoid potential\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "confusion with http:// URLs, the examples in this section use non-resolvable URIs\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "such as urn:example:within. Fortunately, you can just specify this prefix once at the\n",
        "label": "text"
    },
    {
        "text": "top of the file, and then forget about it.",
        "label": "text"
    }
]